prompt: Load the `writing-style` skill from ~/.claude/skills/writing-style/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/writing-style/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[writing-style] applies` on its own line. Then format this passage in my style: "We should arguably leverage the new pipeline to utilize our existing infrastructure. This is a HUGE win for the team! We will circle back on the remaining items due to the fact that they are somewhat lower priority." Output the rewritten passage first (no preamble before it), then a bulleted change log citing the section each edit came from.
expect_substr: [writing-style] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer does two things the skill's Formatter section requires. (1) It outputs a REWRITTEN passage that actually corrects the seeded violations per the guide — the business-speak "leverage" and "circle back" are gone (e.g. "use", "return to"/"follow up on"); the long words are replaced ("utilize" → "use", "due to the fact that" → "because"); the hedges "arguably" and "somewhat" are cut or made definite; the exclamation mark is removed; and "HUGE" in all-caps is de-capitalised (emphasis via bold/italics or plain words, never UPPERCASE). The rewrite reads as plain, active, first-person prose. (2) It then emits a bulleted CHANGE LOG — one bullet per substantive edit, each citing the guide section the rule came from (Vocabulary, Voice, Punctuation, Emphasis, etc.), e.g. "Vocabulary: 'leverage' → 'use'" or "Emphasis: removed UPPERCASE 'HUGE'". Wrong answers to flag: echoing the passage unchanged or nearly so; leaving any of leverage/utilize/circle back/due to the fact that/the exclamation mark/the all-caps word in place; producing a rewrite with NO change log, or a change log that doesn't cite sections; adding a chatty preamble before the rewritten passage instead of leading with the text.
tools: claude,kiro,codex
